4.2 Bug Fixing

During testing, several issues were checked and fixed.

Examples:

Collision detection was tested.

River and log interaction was tested.

Player movement boundaries were checked.

Game restart functionality was tested.

High score saving and loading were checked.

Audio-related problems were checked.

Java compilation and execution errors were fixed.


4.3 Code Structure Improvement

The project was divided into several classes so that each class performs a specific task.

Examples:

Player.java → controls the player.

Car.java → controls cars.

Log.java → controls logs.

Collision.java → handles collision detection.

Score.java → manages score.

GameManager.java → manages game state.

LevelGenerator.java → generates new sections.

TileManager.java → manages grass, road and river tiles.

UIManager.java → manages menu, score and game-over screens.

SaveSystem.java → saves and loads high score.

AudioManager.java → manages game sounds.


This makes the project easier to understand, maintain and modify.

4.4 OOP Principles

Object-Oriented Programming principles were applied in the project.

The game uses separate classes and objects for different game components. For example, Player, Car, Log, and TileManager are separate classes.

This improves modularity, reusability and maintainability of the program.

4.5 Executable Package

The Java project was compiled successfully and an executable JAR package was prepared.

The JAR package contains the compiled game classes and can be used to run the final game using Java.

Example:

ChickenCrossRoad.jar

The project was also tested from the terminal to make sure the compiled game could be executed.

